# The annotations of the BASIC sector (kit/mkgame.py --part src/basic/basic.json). Format: ../../../../docs/ann-format.md.
#
# The image: sector 0 of the file boot (orig/boot.scl, file 0: B, start 180, length 180, 46 sectors), 256 bytes at
# PROG $5D3B, where TR-DOS's RUN "boot" loads the program: only the program's 180 bytes and the 4-byte autostart mark
# ($5D3B-$5DF2); TR-DOS leaves TrdosNextSector $5CF4 at the next sector (c1 frame 446: 01 01). The other 45 sectors are the menu's code, graphics and music, which the loader in line 4 reads from the disk
# itself (docs/03-build-and-load.md).
# Line 10 runs first (the autostart): CLEAR 65347, RANDOMIZE USR 23936 (the loader at $5D80), then RUN, which starts
# the program again at line 1 and so runs the TR-DOS command the menu's launcher wrote into line 1.
# Credit: the names of the loader's steps follow Vadim's 2010 decompilation (work/candidates/sources-2010-vadim,
# boot1.!b is this sector); it has no names for this sector, so every name here is the case's.

@equ $3D13 TrdosEntry ; the TR-DOS ROM's entry point for machine code: C = 5 reads B sectors from track D, sector E to HL
@equ $5CF4 TrdosNextSector 2 ; TR-DOS: the track and sector after the last one read
@equ $61A8 MenuStart 1 ; the menu's code (src/main: Start), 15 sectors
@equ $A600 MenuGraphics 1 ; the font and the graphics (src/main: Font), 8 sectors
@equ $C000 MusicPiece 1 ; the music (src/music), 22 sectors
@const MusicSectors 22 ; the music piece's sectors (a variant's music piece has its own count, variants/)
@isub $5DAE LD BC,MusicSectors*256+5

@module basic ; The BASIC program, its loader in line 4, the autostart mark and the sector's tail
@about Sector 0 of boot, the BASIC program TR-DOS loads at PROG $5D3B and starts at line 10 (the autostart mark $80 $AA $0A $00 after the program). The program's lines: 1 RANDOMIZE USR VAL "15619": REM : RUN "        " (the launch line the menu rewrites); 2 RANDOMIZE USR VAL "15619": REM : RUN; 3 RANDOMIZE USR VAL "15616"; 4 REM with the loader's machine code; 10 BORDER NOT PI: PAPER NOT PI: INK VAL "7": CLEAR VAL "65347": RANDOMIZE USR VAL "23936": RUN.
@about RANDOMIZE USR 15619 is TR-DOS's entry for a command in a BASIC line: the statement after the REM is a TR-DOS command. Lines 2 and 3 are not reached in any recording (line 1's command runs the chosen file and does not come back); line 3, USR 15616, would enter TR-DOS's command line.

$5D3B b LaunchLine 8 ; Line 1: RANDOMIZE USR VAL "15619": REM : RUN "        " (26 bytes): the launch line
> Rewritten by the menu's launcher RunLetter $6A51 just before it returns: the token at $5D4C (LaunchToken) becomes RUN ($F7) for a BASIC file or GO TO ($EC) for a 192-sector CODE file, the 8 characters at $5D4E (LaunchName) the file's name, and the byte at $5D57 (LaunchSuffix) a space for RUN or the token CODE ($AF) for GO TO. Line 10's RUN then starts the program at this line, and TR-DOS runs the command.
> Witness: c2 ran TEST 03 and c3 ran TEST 30 after the menu wrote RUN "T03     " and RUN "T30     " here (the screens at the end of the recordings).
$5D4C LaunchToken ; the command token: RUN ($F7) in the image
$5D4D ; the opening quote
$5D4E LaunchName ; the file name, 8 characters, spaces in the image
$5D56 ; the closing quote
$5D57 LaunchSuffix ; a space, or the token CODE ($AF) for a GO TO
$5D58 ; the line's end ($0D)
@res basic/launch-lines.bin

$5D59 b LineTwo 8 ; Line 2: RANDOMIZE USR VAL "15619": REM : RUN (15 bytes; not reached in any recording)
> A TR-DOS RUN without a name runs "boot" again. The program only gets here if line 1's command returns to BASIC without an error; no recording does.
@res basic/launch-lines.bin

$5D6C b LineThree 8 ; Line 3: RANDOMIZE USR VAL "15616" (11 bytes; not reached in any recording)
> USR 15616 enters TR-DOS's command line (the A> prompt).
@res basic/launch-lines.bin

$5D7B b LineFour 5 ; Line 4: the header (line number, length 71) and the REM token before the loader
> The loader's code follows the REM token $EA at $5D7F; the line's $0D is at $5DC5.

$5D80 c Loader ; Clear the menu's memory, read the code, the graphics and the music from the disk, run the menu, clear again
> Entered by line 10's RANDOMIZE USR 23936 with TR-DOS's TrdosNextSector $5CF4 pointing at the sector after sector 0 of boot (TR-DOS just loaded the program). Clears $61A8-$FEAF (LDIR of $9D08 bytes), then three TrdosEntry $3D13 reads (C = 5), each starting at TrdosNextSector: 15 sectors to MenuStart $61A8 ($61A8-$70A7), 8 sectors to MenuGraphics $A600 ($A600-$ADFF), 22 sectors to MusicPiece $C000 ($C000-$D5FF). Then CALL MenuStart: the menu returns here when a file is chosen, with the launch line already rewritten. Clears $61A8-$FEAF again and returns to BASIC, which goes on with line 10's RUN.
> The memory is cleared because the menu builds its tables and buffers in the zero bytes between its pieces ($70A8-$A5FF, $AE00-$BFFF, $D600-$FEAF) and the program it starts should find clean memory.
> Witness: L1 4 (c1-c4).
$5D8D ; the code: 15 sectors from the sector after the BASIC
$5D9A ; the font and the graphics: 8 sectors
$5DA7 ; the music: 22 sectors
$5DB4 ; the menu: returns once a file is chosen (RunLetter)
$5DB7 ; clear the menu's memory again, then RET to BASIC

$5DC5 b LineFourEnd 1 ; Line 4's end ($0D)

$5DC6 b LineTen 8 ; Line 10: BORDER NOT PI: PAPER NOT PI: INK VAL "7": CLEAR VAL "65347": RANDOMIZE USR VAL "23936": RUN
> The autostart line: black border and paper, white ink, RAMTOP $FF43 (the menu's stack $FCBC and IM 2 table $FD00-$FE00 lie below it), the loader $5D80, then RUN, which clears the variables and runs line 1.
@res basic/line-ten.bin

$5DEF b AutostartMark 4 ; The program's end ($80) and TR-DOS's autostart mark: $AA and the line, 10
> TR-DOS writes the line number after $AA when a program is saved with LINE; RUN "boot" starts the program there.
@res basic/line-ten.bin

$5DF3 b SectorTail 8 ; The sector's leftover bytes after the program: not loaded, not read
> 72 bytes that were in memory after the program when it was saved: `oot27" LINE 10` reads like the end of a `SAVE "boot27" LINE 10` statement, followed by number bytes and zeros. TR-DOS does not load them (it loads only the program and the autostart mark, $5D3B-$5DF2: at c1 frame 446 $5E07-$5E3A are zeros), and the Loader starts at the next sector, so they stay on the disk; the build reproduces them because the file holds them. A resource (spare/basic-tail.bin, bytes): in most variants they name another SAVE (`boot12`, `boot32`).
@res spare/basic-tail.bin
